    <string name="shadow_not_connected">Kernel module not connected</string>
    <string name="shadow_need_root">Root is required to read the token</string>
    <string name="shadow_health_ok">Elevation succeeded (this design never reverts; reverted=0 is normal)</string>
    <string name="shadow_health_leak">Counter mismatch (this design never reverts; reverted=0 is normal)</string>
    <string name="shadow_copied">Token copied</string>

    <string name="tab_shadow">Shadow User</string>
    <string name="tab_launcher">Launcher</string>
    <string name="shadow_intro_title">What is a Shadow User</string>
    <string name="shadow_intro_body">A traditional superuser hands an app a root identity: the app spawns a uid 0 process and keeps it. That process is visible to ps and easy to detect.

A shadow user is different. The app never changes its uid. Privileges are elevated only inside the one system call that actually needs them, then reverted immediately. ps shows nothing unusual, and no su binary or allowlist file exists.

In short: a traditional superuser answers "who are you", a shadow user answers "on whose behalf does this one call run".</string>
    <string name="welcome_title">Welcome to Umbral</string>
    <string name="welcome_body">Umbral is a fourth generation root framework built around shadow privilege.

· Shadow User — grant an app shadow privilege without ever giving it uid 0
· Kernel module — the privileged core, reporting backend and runtime stats
· Modules / KPM — kernel module slots
· Launcher — run .sh and other files with shadow privilege

Use the power button to patch and flash; the menu on the right opens device and about info.</string>
