    <string name="shadow_su_title">Relation to the traditional superuser</string>
    <string name="shadow_su_body">A traditional superuser (su) gives a process uid 0. Any tool that expects su works, but a uid 0 process is trivially detected.

Shadow privilege takes the other route: the process keeps its own uid, and only the syscalls that need privilege are elevated for their duration.

Tools written against su (file managers in root mode, terminals, shell scripts that call su) do not work under shadow privilege alone — they need a real su channel. Apps written against the shadow API do not need su at all.</string>

