    <string name="rootuser_what_title">What superuser means</string>
    <string name="rootuser_what_body">Superuser (su) is the traditional way to elevate: an app asks for root, and once granted its process uid really becomes 0, giving it sight and control over everything on the device — with no boundary at all.\n\nBecause there is no boundary, one compromised, mistyped or remotely exploited root app costs you the whole device. That is exactly why shadow privilege exists.</string>
    <string name="rootuser_compare_title">Versus shadow user</string>
    <string name="rootuser_compare_body">· Process uid\n    Superuser: actually 0, until the process exits\n    Shadow: unchanged, only selected syscalls are allowed\n\n· Scope\n    Superuser: everything\n    Shadow: only what the window allows\n\n· Kernel records\n    Superuser: keeps an allowlist\n    Shadow: keeps nothing, only an in-memory token\n\n· Detection\n    Superuser: easy, uid gives it away\n    Shadow: hard, the process still sees its own uid\n\n· Across exec\n    Superuser: persists\n    Shadow: currently lost (path B pending)\n\n· Can run su tooling\n    Superuser: yes\n    Shadow: no</string>
    <string name="rootuser_when_title">Which one to use</string>
    <string name="rootuser_when_body">MT Manager root mode, Termux, or any script written against su → use superuser.\n\nLetting one app read a config under /data without handing it the whole device → use shadow.\n\nThey do not conflict. Being honest though: the superuser channel is not wired up yet — the manager itself has no root, so this page can only read status, not manage the allowlist.</string>

